=======
libkrun
=======

Quirks
======

Because of the way rust dependencies are vendored maintenance of this package
is a little special.

We are roughly following the official guide to rust vendoring from:
https://github.com/canonical/ubuntu-mir/blob/main/vendoring/Rust.md

In addition it is recommended to use git-buildpackage(1) to manage patches and
update releases from git.

An orig tarball including the vendored rust dependencies can be generated via
`./debian/rules vendor-tarball`

Updating
========

Make sure your local git checkout has a remote for the ubuntu package repo and
one for the upstream repo configured.
Before you update it makes sense to create a patch-queue branch using
`gbp pq import`.
You can switch between patch-queue and regular branches using `gbp pq switch`.

First fetch the new release tags from the upstream remote:

```
$ git fetch upstream
 ...
From https://github.com/containers/libkrun
 * [new tag]	v1.10.0

```

You can use `gbp import-ref` to merge this new tag into your packaging branch:

```
$ gbp import-reg -u v1.10.0
```

After this the vendor directory will have to be regenerated to update the rust
dependencies:

```
$ ./debian/rules vendor-deps
```

And the resulting rust-vendor directory might have to be committed to git so
that we can modify it with our patches.

You can then rebase the patch-queue branch onto the new base to update the
patches and make sure they still apply:

```
$ gbp pq rebase 
# You are now on patch-queue/ubuntu/$release branch
```

While we are there, generate a new list of vendored dependencies for d/control:

```
$ CARGO_VENDOR_DIR=rust-vendor /usr/share/cargo/bin/dh-cargo-vendored-sources
```
And copy the output somewhere for later.

If everything went well export and commit the new patches:
```
$ gbp pq export
# This takes you back to ubuntu/$release

$ git add debian/patches
$ git commit

```

Now update d/control with the dependency list generated previously.

Finally use `gbp dch -R` to generate a changelog entry from the new git commits.

# Building

Generate the regular tarball and then switch to patched branch and generate the
new vendored tarball with:

```
$ gbp export-orig
$ gbp pq switch # to patch-queue/ubuntu/*
$ ./debian/rules vendor-tarball
$ gbp pq switch # back to ubuntu/*
```

It is important to generate the vendor tarball from the patched sources
because the patches modify the rust build files to be compatible.

After switching back to your main packaging branch, build using sbuild(1),
gbp-buildpackage(1) or dpkg-buildpackage(1).
